course: dictionary
lesson: 4403
title: Old weapons, old words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to the past. You can still work out their meaning from the words around them. «The chasing engine is full of men like ancient warders, waving halberds» tells you that warders are guards, and that they carry something long to wave about. Ancient means very old, so these guards come from long ago.
explain: The writer lists other things the men wave: truncheons, which are police clubs, and revolvers, which are guns. A halberd is in the same list, so it is a weapon. The brackets after the sentence give the meaning straight away: a long pole with an axe-blade. Try the meaning in the sentence to check it works.
explain: The writer chose old weapons on purpose. Because the halberds are so old-fashioned, the scene feels ancient and frightening, as if the story has stepped back into the past.
fact: A halberd is a long wooden pole with an axe blade, a sharp spike on top and often a hook at the back.
fact: Halberds were used by soldiers from about the 1300s to the 1600s.
fact: Today, some guards carry halberds as ceremonial weapons, such as the Swiss Guard at the Vatican.
fact: A soldier who carries a halberd is called a halberdier.
exercise: «Men like ancient warders, waving halberds» – What is a halberd? | A tall horse with a long tail | A bell rung in a tower | *A long pole with an axe blade and a sharp spike on top | A small knife for cutting bread
exercise: Which word in «Men like ancient warders, waving halberds» tells you the warders are from long ago? | halberds | *ancient | waving | warders
exercise: Which word means a soldier who carries a halberd? | a detective | a policeman | *a halberdier
test: «... warders waving halberds, policemen waving truncheons and detectives waving revolvers» – What is a truncheon? | A sharp spike on top of a pole | A flower that grows in a castle garden | A type of bowler hat | *A police club
test: Today, where might you see a halberd carried? | In a school gym as a sports prop | *By some guards as a ceremonial weapon, such as the Swiss Guard at the Vatican | In a supermarket on the shelves | On a farm, to move hay bales
exam: Why do the old weapons in the story, such as halberds, make the scene feel so frightening? | Because the guards are kind and friendly | Because they are shiny and new | Because they are used to cook food | *Because they are ancient, which makes the scene feel like it is from the past and dangerous
